---
id: a7e7d5b8-d681-4636-9d83-a833fda4ae30
title: Iron Deficiency Anaemia
---

# Iron Deficiency Anaemia

- **Definition** - anaemia as a result of deficient total body iron occuring when there is iron loss (via blood loss), or physiological requirements exceed absorption
- **Pathophysiology of iron deficiency anaemia:**
  - <u>Normal iron stores</u> - 8-10 x serum ferritin (ng/mL)
  - <u>Progressive iron depletion</u>:
    - **Early phase** - reduced ferritin (but may be masked in liver disease and APP)
    - **Functional iron deficiency** - compatible iron deficiency changes +/- mild Nc anaemia
    - **Iron deficiency anaemia** - McHc anaemia with compatible iron studies
- **Etiology of iron deficiency anaemia:**
  - **Blood loss:**
    - <u>GI bleeding</u> - UGIB and LGIB (often exacerbated by aspirin and NSAID) is the most common explanation for occult blood loss in men and post-menopausal women
    - <u>Menstrual blood loss</u> - most common explanation for blood loss in pre-menopausal women (1/3 of pre-menopausal women have low iron stores while 3% presnet w/ iron-deficient haematopoiesis)
    - <u>Haemoptysis and haematuria</u> - very unlikely to cause iron deficiency because of its alarming presentation
  - **Malabsorption** - iron deficiency anaemia may arise from reduced iron intake or impaired absorption:
    - <u>Achlorohydria</u> - achlorohydria from past **gastric surgery**, drugs (e.g. PPI) or idiopathic ahydrochloryia of the elderly may result in impaired iron absorption as H+ is neccessary to keep iron in soluble ferrous state
    - <u>Bowel disease</u> - other causes of malabsorption e.g. coeliac disease, Crohn's disease
  - **Increased physiological demand:**
    - <u>Growth</u> - during infancy and puberty, iron requirements increase and may outstrip absorption
    - <u>Pregnancy</u> - iron diverted to the fetus, the placenta, and will be subsequently lost w/ bleeding at parturition
- **Approach to iron deficiency anaemia** - Iron deficiency anaemia is not a diagnosis on its own:
  - Hx - menorrhagia, GI bleeding, bleeding disorders, previous blood donation, drug Hx (bleeding tendancies), risk factors for GI cancers, previous scopes
  - P/E - Abdomen and LN palpation
  - Investigation for occult GI bleeding:
    - Indications - older men and post-menopausal women
    - FOBT
    - Stool examination
    - Upper endoscopy then colonoscopy
    - SB work up if endoscopy -ve
- **Ix** - 1) confirmation of iron deficiency anaemia, and 2) investigation of cause:
  - **CBC:**
    - <u>Hb and red cell indices</u> - microcytic (low MCV), hypochromic (low MCHC) anaemia
    - <u>PLT</u> - reactive thrombocytosis in response to occult bleeding
  - **Iron profile** - serum iron, TIBC, %Tf, and ferritin:
    - <u>Plasma iron</u> - reduced
    - <u>TIBC</u> - increased
    - <u>%Tf</u> - reduced
    - <u>Ferritin</u> - reduced

    
    ![](../img/iron_studies_for_iron_deficiency_anaemia_and_anaemia_of_chronic_illness.png)
  - **Soluble transferrin receptors** - not widely available:
    - <u>Rationale</u> - up-regulation of transferrin receptors in times of poor iron store
    - <u>Interpretation</u> - raised level reflects iron deficiency anaemia
  - **Upper and lower GI workup** - OGD and colonoscopy in men and post-menopausal women (+/- endomysial or anti-transglutaminase Ab coeliac disease)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Transfusion</u> - restrictive transfusion strategy (\< 7g/dL) unless patient has existing heart disease, or patient has angina, heart failure or evidence of cerebral hypoxia
    - <u>Iron replacement</u> - oral iron replacement for replenishment of iron stores
  - **Iron replacement:**
    - <u>Regimen</u>:
      - Ferrous sulphate 200 mg tid (195 mg of elemental iron per day)
      - Ferrous gluconate 300 mg bid (70 mg of elemental iron per day)
    - <u>S/E</u> - GI side effects more likely w/ ferrous sulphate:
      - Dyspepsia
      - Altered bowel habits
    - <u>Clinical efficacy</u>:
      - Hb expected to rise by 1g/dL every 7-10d
      - Reticulocyte response evident within a week
    - <u>Failure to respond</u> - may be due to:
      - Non compliance
      - Continued blood loss
      - Malabsorption (may require parenteral iron therapy)
      - Incorrect diagnosis
